{"attack": true,"case": "The target's core move — claiming that preferring better-corroborated theories is 'not inductive' but rather 'recognizing an existing dispositional property' — is circular. To say a theory has an 'objective propensity to survive specific test conditions' is meaningless unless we can ascertain that propensity's value, and the only available evidence for it is the theory's observed track record of surviving past tests. Inferring a future-relevant dispositional property from past observations is precisely induction. The target relabels the inductive step as 'recognition' without supplying any non-inductive epistemic route to the propensity value, so it presupposes what it set out to replace.","premise": "The target asserts that preferring higher corroboration is non-inductive because it recognizes a dispositional property, but provides no mechanism for knowing that property's value other than inference from past test outcomes.","premise_evidence": [{"block": "4ee0f7c5809e536ed51a291898ae0bdc69f4ff1197fb4efa3825cc8ad4c54491","quote": "Preferring the higher propensity is not inductive (predicting the future from the past) but recognizing an existing dispositional property of the theory relative to the world."}],"counterexample": ["A theory T has survived 1000 tests. The target says we recognize T's 'propensity to survive tests' — but the only ground for assigning T a high propensity rather than a low one is that it survived those 1000 tests. That inference from past survival to current dispositional strength is structurally identical to the induction the target claims to avoid. Relabeling it 'recognition' does not change the inferential structure."],"successor_question": "If the propensity to survive tests cannot be known without inductively inferring it from past test outcomes, in what substantive sense is preferring the better-corroborated theory non-inductive rather than induction under a different name?"}